<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#greek-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greek language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greek language is an Indo-European language with a written history stretching back over three thousand years. After the conquests of Alexander it became a common language of administration and culture across the Macedonian and Seleucid realms. It was the main language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and was also used in inscriptions and coinage by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greek-language.jpg" alt="Greek language" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrikkos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pater_noster_in_greek_script.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Greek language</b> and the classical philosophical, literary and scientific heritage preserved in Byzantine libraries, transmitted to Italy by scholars such as Manuel Chrysoloras from 1397 and by émigrés after the empire's fall, helped shape the Italian Renaissance: the recovery of Plato and the Greek historians in Florence,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His army, coinage and standing force were the instruments with which Alexander overthrew the Achaemenid Empire and carried <b>Greek language</b> and culture to the Indus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The ruling class mixed Persian aristocratic traditions, including Iranian names and cults, with <b>Greek language</b> and culture, and royal coins and inscriptions were in Greek.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Greek language</b>, art and philosophy spread across Egypt, Mesopotamia, Persia and Central Asia, influencing government, literature and science for centuries, and Koine Greek became a common language of administration and commerce.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…gymnasium and the theatre, into landscapes with no previous tradition of this kind of civic organisation, and the <b>Greek language</b> they propagated became a medium of administration, commerce and intellectual life across the Near East for centuries, persisting as the language of the early Christian church, Byzantine administration…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In eastern Crete the Eteocretans, the “true Cretans”, wrote a non-<b>Greek language</b> in the Greek alphabet until the 3rd century BCE, perhaps a descendant of the Minoan language, though this cannot be proved.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
